%S A397786 1,0,1,1,1,1,0,1,3,1,2,0,0,1,5,1,2,1,0,1,4,1,1,4,1,0,1,9,1,4,1,0,0,0,
%T A397786 1,11,1,6,2,0,1,6,1,1,1,4,2,1,0,1,15,1,5,2,1,0,0,1,17,1,6,2,0,1,0,1,
%U A397786 10,2,1,1,10,4,0,1,21,1,5,2,1,1,0,0,0,1,16,3,1
%N A397786 T(n,k) is the number of positive integers x such that there are exactly k divisors d of n with x<=d and gcd(x,d)=1, 1 <= k <= A000005(n), read by rows.
%C A397786 There is a beautiful identity: Sum_{d|n} phi(d) = n, where phi is the Euler totient function.
%C A397786 This identity can be expressed by writing n as a sum, for each x<=n, of the number of divisors of n such that x<=d and x and d are relatively prime.
%C A397786 For example letting x range from 1 to 8 we get 8 = card{1,2,4,8} + card({}) + card({4,8}) + card({}) + card({8}) + card({}) + card({8}) + card({}) or 8 = 4+0+2+0+1+0+1+0. Notice this expression of 8 as the sum of eight numbers has two 1s, one 2, and one 4.
%C A397786 As two more examples we have 9 = 3+2+0+1+1+0+1+1+0, a sum with four 1s, one 2 and one 3, and we have 10 = 4+1+2+1+0+0+1+0+1+0, with four 1s, one 2 and one 4.
%C A397786 The n-th row of the following array counts the number of 1s, 2s, 3s, etc appearing in these sums.
%C A397786 So from the examples above we have the 8th, 9th and 10th rows being
%C A397786   2, 1, 0, 4, 0, 0, 0, 0...
%C A397786   4, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0... and
%C A397786   4, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0... respectively.
%C A397786 If we treat A as the infinite lower-diagonal matrix with entries T(n,k) in the n-th row and k-th column, and we treat the natural numbers N as a column vector, then N is an eigenvector of A with eigenvalue 1, that is A*N=N. This gives another formulation of the totient identity. For example dotting the tenth row with N gives <4, 1, 0, 1, 0...><1, 2, 3, 4, 5...> = 4+2+0+4 = 10.
%F A397786 T(p,1) = p-2 for prime p.
%F A397786 T(n, d(n)) = 1, where d(n) = A000005(n).
%F A397786 Sum_{k>=1} k*T(n,k) = n.
%e A397786 A(4,3) = card({x in N : card({d>=x: d|4, gcd(x,d)=1})=3}) = card({1}) because card({d>=1: d|4, gcd(1,d)=1})=card({1,2,4})=3.
%e A397786 A(7,1) = card({x in N : card({d>=x: d|7, gcd(x,d)=1})=1}) = card({2,3,4,5,6}) = 5.
%e A397786 The nonzero support for the n-th line lies in the first d(n) columns.
%e A397786 The triangle begins:
%e A397786    1 |  1;
%e A397786    2 |  0, 1;
%e A397786    3 |  1, 1;
%e A397786    4 |  1, 0, 1;
%e A397786    5 |  3, 1;
%e A397786    6 |  2, 0, 0, 1;
%e A397786    7 |  5, 1;
%e A397786    8 |  2, 1, 0, 1;
%e A397786    9 |  4, 1, 1;
%e A397786   10 |  4, 1, 0, 1;
%e A397786   11 |  9, 1;
%e A397786   12 |  4, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1;
%e A397786   13 | 11, 1;
%e A397786   14 |  6, 2, 0, 1;
%e A397786   15 |  6, 1, 1, 1;
%e A397786   16 |  4, 2, 1, 0, 1;
%e A397786   17 | 15, 1;
%e A397786   18 |  5, 2, 1, 0, 0, 1;
%e A397786   19 | 17, 1;
%e A397786   20 |  6, 2, 0, 1, 0, 1;
%e A397786     ...
%o A397786 (SageMath)
%o A397786 def a(n,k):
%o A397786      xs=[x for x in range(1,n+1) if len([d for d in range(1,n+1) if (n%d==0 and x<=d and gcd(x,d)==1)])==k]
%o A397786      return len(xs)
%o A397786 (PARI)
%o A397786 row(n) = {my(v=vector(numdiv(n))); for(x=1, n, my(k=sumdiv(n, d, gcd(x,d)==1 && x<=d)); if(k, v[k]++)); v}
%o A397786 { for(n=1, 20, print(row(n))) } \\ _Andrew Howroyd_, Aug 07 2026
%Y A397786 Columns 1..2 are A398171, A397318.
